From: Waltteri Hooli <1420194+wahooli@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] local: remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_pas: unbind q6prm before releasing the GLINK edge

On 7.3 every hibernation resets the SoC (PS_HOLD) inside the
PM_HIBERNATION_PREPARE teardown of the ADSP edge. pm_test=freezer
reproduces it on a fresh boot; hibernate_restart=N avoids it.

qcom_glink_native_remove() sets abort_tx before it removes its rpmsg
children, so every GPR request made during the q6prm consumer walk fails.
That was harmless while the LPASS macros held their macro and dcodec votes
from probe. 7.3 moved them to pm_clk: idle macros drop every vote, the
walk runtime-resumes them, pm_clk_resume() logs the failed enable and
returns 0, and regcache_sync() writes unclocked LPASS registers.

Unbind q6prm first, while GPR still answers. The driver core then runs the
same consumer walk with working clocks, and the edge removal that follows
finds q6prm already unbound. Unbinding qcom-prm by hand before a
pm_test=freezer hibernation turned the reset into a clean return.

--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c
@@ -959,6 +959,22 @@
 };
 
 /*
+ * Edge removal fails every send before it walks its children, and the LPASS
+ * macros clock themselves through pm_clk, which ignores a failed enable. Left
+ * to the edge, the q6prm consumer walk writes unclocked LPASS registers and
+ * the firmware resets the SoC, so unbind q6prm while GPR still answers.
+ */
+static int qcom_pas_unbind_q6prm(struct device *dev, void *data)
+{
+	if (of_device_is_compatible(dev->of_node, "qcom,q6prm")) {
+		device_release_driver(dev);
+		return 1;
+	}
+
+	return device_for_each_child(dev, NULL, qcom_pas_unbind_q6prm);
+}
+
+/*
  * Release the attach-only edge before the snapshot rather than after it.
  *
  * Removing the edge removes its rpmsg children, and the audio one pulls the
@@ -968,11 +984,10 @@
  * in device_release_driver_internal() on one of those codecs and did not come
  * back.
  *
- * Here the DSP is still the one this image has been talking to, GPR still
- * answers, and PM_HIBERNATION_PREPARE runs before freeze_processes(), so
- * userspace can still close its ALSA files. That makes this the same teardown
- * the SSR path takes, which 0099-0104 already made survivable -- and a stall
- * here leaves a live machine with a shell and a journal.
+ * Here the DSP is still the one this image has been talking to, and
+ * PM_HIBERNATION_PREPARE runs before freeze_processes(), so userspace can still
+ * close its ALSA files. That makes this the same teardown the SSR path takes,
+ * which 0099-0104 already made survivable.
  */
 static void qcom_pas_glink_teardown(struct qcom_pas *pas)
 {
@@ -989,6 +1004,8 @@
 	if (!rproc->ops->start && rproc->state == RPROC_ATTACHED &&
 	    !IS_ERR_OR_NULL(glink->edge)) {
 		dev_info(pas->dev, "hibernate: releasing GLINK before the image\n");
+		if (device_for_each_child(&rproc->dev, NULL, qcom_pas_unbind_q6prm))
+			dev_info(pas->dev, "hibernate: q6prm unbound while GPR still answers\n");
 		qcom_glink_smem_unregister(glink->edge);
 		glink->edge = NULL;
 		pas->hibernate_glink_torn = true;
